Define a piecewise linear function by
\[f(x) = \begin{cases} x & \text{if } x \in [-1, 1) \\ 2 - x & \text{if } x \in [1, 3)\end{cases}\]
together with $f(x + 4) = f(x)$ for all real numbers $x.$ Its graph is the sawtooth shown below. 

\begin{asy}
import graph;

size(18cm);
real f(real x) {
    real x_mod = x - 4 * floor((x+1) / 4);
    if (-1 <= x_mod && x_mod < 1) {
        return x_mod;
    } else if (1 <= x_mod && x_mod < 3) {
        return 2 - x_mod;
    }
    return 0; // Should never reach here
}

// Set up axes
real xmin = -8, xmax = 11, ymin = -2, ymax = 2;
xaxis("", xmin, -2, Ticks(2.0));
xaxis("", -2, 2);
xaxis("", 2, xmax, Ticks(2.0));
yaxis("", ymin, ymax, Ticks(2.0));
// Plot the function in segments due to periodicity
real step = 0.01;
for (real k = -8; k < 7; k += 4) {
    draw(graph(f, k, k + 4, n=(int)((4)/step)),blue);
}
    draw(graph(f, 8, 11, n=(int)((4)/step)),blue);
\end{asy}

The parabola $x = 34y^2$ meets the graph of $f(x)$ in finitely many points. The sum of the $y$-coordinates of these points can be written as $\tfrac{a + b\sqrt c}d,$ where $a, b, c$ and $d$ are positive integers, $a, b,$ and $d$ has greatest common divisor equal to $1,$ and $c$ is not divisible by the square of any prime. Find $a + b + c + d.$

